 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, … (1², 2², 3², …). The square root of 49 is 7, because 7² = 49. We write √49 = 7.\nCube numbers: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … (1³, 2³, 3³, …). The cube root of 64 is 4, because 4³ = 64. We write ∛64 = 4.\nSquare roots with prime factors: halve every index. 144 = 2⁴ × 3², so √144 = 2² × 3 = 12. For a cube root, divide every index by 3.\nA number that is not a square has a square root between two whole numbers: 25 < 30 < 36, so √30 is between 5 and 6.\nA square of area A has sides of length √A.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": 324
  },
  "problem": "Find √{n} using prime factors.",
  "steps": [
   "Write {n} in index form: {join(ladder(n), '; ')}, so {n} = {factorise(n)}.",
   "Halve every index: √{n} = {factorise(18)}.",
   "{factorise(18)} = {18}."
  ],
  "answer": "√{n} = {18}",
  "checks": [
   "18 * 18 == n"
  ]
 },
